WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:14.320
And our feature presenter today,
is Professor Bernie Roth.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:19.260
Bernie is the Rodney H Adams, professor
of engineering at Stanford University.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:23.040
He earned his PhD in Mechanical
Engineering from Columbia University.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:27.030
And has honorary PhD's from the University
of Paris, and the University of Cassino.

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:31.640
He has published widely in
the areas of design, creativity,

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:34.430
education, kinematics, and robotics.

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:37.920
And has many awards for
both his teaching and research.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.250
He has served as a director for several
corporations and has held top leadership

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:45.790
positions in several international
professional societies.

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:49.570
He is also a co-founder of Stanford's
d.school where he now serves as

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:51.600
the Academic Director.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:53.989
And his latest book,
The Achievement Habit,

00:00:53.989 --> 00:00:57.210
was published in July
2015 by HarperCollins.

00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:00.000
And now I'd like to turn
the floor over to Bernie.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.720
&gt;&gt; Hello, everybody, thanks for

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:02.650
joining in.

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:06.590
And I would say good morning or good
afternoon, but it depends where you are.

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:08.030
So whatever it is, good to you.

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:14.140
I just want to start off by
saying something about the title.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:18.100
And the title has the word
success in it and achieve in it.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:24.300
And I used these words in
a kind of unusual way in that

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:29.280
most people think success means becoming
emperor or empress of the universe.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.889
What I mean by them is that
you have a good life and

00:01:31.889 --> 00:01:36.050
that you feel good about yourself and
people feel good about you.

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:41.380
And basically when you die, friends
that eulogize you don't have to lie.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:45.100
You can define what all that means for
you personally.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:48.020
But the main thing we're
going to talk about is problems.

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:53.430
And I think it's important to understand
something about what I mean by problems.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.569
And for me, problems are a life force.

00:01:56.830 --> 00:01:58.460
It's actually what makes us tick.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:04.740
There's actually a story I like very
much that's a novel written in the 1860s

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.920
in Russia, in Czarist Russia.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:13.240
And the main character's name is Oblomov,
and that's the title of the book.

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:16.470
And for
the first 200 some odd pages of the book,

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:19.980
the main character never leaves his bed.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:22.710
And it's because he's
a very wealthy person and

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:24.970
everyone takes care of everything for him.

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:27.150
And the idea is that if
you have no problems,

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:29.890
you have nothing to do,
why get out of bed?

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:34.820
And of course the book is
just a critical statement

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:39.080
about the life of the aristocracy
in czarist Russia.

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:41.560
But I think it's a really important point.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:46.260
I find that when I have problems,
have things I'm concerned about doing,

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:49.250
it's an exciting part of my life.

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:51.600
It's kind of what makes me tick.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.740
And I think we all do it that way,
we may or

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:58.280
may not think of it that way
consciously but it's important.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:02.920
So for me the problem with the word
problems is that it sounds bad.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:04.530
When, in fact, it's good.

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:09.930
So I think that if you understand that,
the kind of people around me who

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:15.350
are big on entrepreneurship and all that,
they say problems are opportunities.

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:18.800
And the bigger the problem,
the bigger the opportunity.

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:23.630
Now that may be an extreme view for
you, but it is actually true that

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:30.370
solving problems is a pleasure and it's
actually necessary to get along in life.

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:34.840
Now most of us do a good job at it and
occasionally we get stumped and I'm going

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:39.210
to talk to you about what happens when
we get stumped a little later on.

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:43.400
But first I want to talk to you
about successful problem solving for

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:46.700
others because it's easy if
we look at how we do it for

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:50.970
others to understand what we don't
do right when we do for ourselves.

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:54.330
So one of the things we do
when we solve problems for

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:57.640
others is we come up
with a problem statement.

00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:03.290
I prefer using the word point of view,
rather than a problem statement,

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:08.240
because it implies that in fact the thing
that we're working on is subjective.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:10.180
It's not an objective reality.

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:12.730
So we say problem statement.

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:16.290
Implies more something
that's objectively given.

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:20.380
In fact, all the time there's
a subjective view to it.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:24.440
And the way we do it is we get
a phrase describing a specific user,

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:26.510
which is a very important point.

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:28.370
Who we're solving the problem for.

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:34.130
That a phrase specifying a need and then a
phrase giving an insight specifying what,

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:37.160
but not how the solution
needs to accomplish.

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:41.590
And I'll give you a specific example
that's to make it more concrete.

00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:44.150
So in a course I taught,

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:48.040
the students came up with a problem
statement which was Maria,

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:53.650
a poor single mother, needs financial know
how so she can use her money efficiently.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:59.200
Now if you look at that statement,
Maria's what it's about, there's a need,

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:04.080
financial know how, and what it would
accomplish would be to get her money,

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:05.870
get her to use her money efficiently.

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:08.190
So that's kind of the framing of it.

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:12.390
And when you see that, you can see there
is an insight implied in in here and

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:16.540
that is if she gets more knowledge she's
going to do better with some money.

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:18.060
Now that's an assumption.

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:19.240
And it may not be true.

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:23.195
She may be spendthrift as she may know
everything there is to know about money

00:05:23.195 --> 00:05:25.015
and still not do well about it.

00:05:25.015 --> 00:05:26.655
So you have this assumption, and

00:05:26.655 --> 00:05:29.375
you have to test it if you're
going to solve the problem.

00:05:29.375 --> 00:05:33.722
In this case, the students worked
with some janitors at Stanford.

00:05:33.722 --> 00:05:36.612
And they tested their insight.

00:05:36.612 --> 00:05:41.512
And they found that giving some,
a small amount of knowledge,

00:05:41.512 --> 00:05:43.122
changed these people's lives.

00:05:43.122 --> 00:05:47.652
That this woman was able to save $1400 in

00:05:47.652 --> 00:05:50.762
six months on a salary
of less than $40,000.

00:05:50.762 --> 00:05:57.370
So she was saving 9% of her income,
which she had never done before.

00:05:57.370 --> 00:06:02.500
But by virtue of them getting
her access to banking,

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:06.510
by virtue of them giving her some
knowledge, and things like that.

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:11.510
So that was, in fact,
a life-changing thing, both for the people

00:06:11.510 --> 00:06:15.210
that the problem was solved for and also
for the people who solved the problem,

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:18.600
because they went and
made a company based on that insight.

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:20.260
And the company did very well.

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:22.260
It's won all sorts of awards.

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:26.000
And basically what the company
does it ties people

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:31.000
to financial institutions using
a cell phone, not a smartphone,

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:35.740
just an ordinary cell phone, and
speaks to them in their own language.

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:40.520
And it's been so successful that
the company is called Juntos,

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:42.600
which in Spanish means together.

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:46.850
And now they're in 12 countries
across 4 continents dealing with

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:48.820
some of the biggest banks in the world.

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:53.290
So this is just an example of how
if you have the right problem,

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:56.940
sort of magic can happen if you pursue it.

00:06:56.940 --> 00:07:00.990
And that's kind of the point of what I'm
going to talk about to you, yourself and

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:02.730
your own personal life.

00:07:02.730 --> 00:07:08.370
And the so question that we're
really concern about is not so

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:10.740
much what was done in classes and

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:14.114
the more formal methods we used in
what we called design thinking.

00:07:14.114 --> 00:07:20.110
But problem solving for yourself,
applying the same ideas and techniques.

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:23.270
And so what are we going to do?

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:25.480
How come, do you ever think of this?

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:28.790
I haven't met you, but
I know you're intelligent.

00:07:28.790 --> 00:07:31.280
You're intelligent enough to sign up for
this webinar,

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:34.930
so that shows you're
a highly intelligent person.

00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:38.990
And how come people like you, and
people that I work with every day,

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:42.130
and I, myself, and
people in my family, and

00:07:42.130 --> 00:07:47.140
my friends we get stuck and
we lose sleep over simple problems?

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:50.430
They're not like rocket
science problems as we say.

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:56.320
And they're not generally, things like
peace in the world forever and you know.

00:07:56.320 --> 00:07:57.860
Global issues.

00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:02.850
We generally lose sleep over very
small problems which have to do

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:07.690
with our relationships with our families,
with our work,

00:08:07.690 --> 00:08:12.550
with the small everyday
issues of our lives.

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:14.900
How come, you've solve harder problems?

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:18.480
Everyone I work with has
solved harder problems.

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:21.300
How come you're stuck
with problems like that?

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:25.990
And I've talked to thousands
of people over the years, and

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:31.250
everyone really has problems
that they lose sleep over.

00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:34.250
I only met one person actually,

00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:38.400
who claims he didn't have
any problems falling asleep.

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:42.540
He happens to be the youngest mayor
of the city of Stockton right now.

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:46.320
I haven't checked with him recently, but
I'm sure he's losing sleep over something.

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:48.490
So I think it's universal.

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:51.520
So what can go wrong?

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:52.460
In most cases,

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:57.750
what goes wrong is you're using an old
answer instead of a new question.

00:08:57.750 --> 00:08:59.910
You think you're dealing
with the question, but

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:01.190
you're dealing with an answer.

00:09:02.210 --> 00:09:05.370
I'll give you a simple example of
that that happened in my life.

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:09.210
It's a very trivial example, but
it makes the point very nicely.

00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:16.210
I had a friend visiting me and I wanted
to take him up to the wine country and

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:20.090
I didn't have a car available,
so I rented a car.

00:09:20.090 --> 00:09:23.960
We spent a nice time and eventually,

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:28.510
we're running out of gasoline,
so we went to gas station.

00:09:28.510 --> 00:09:31.530
At that point, the drama started,
I went to look for

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:35.730
the button that releases
the cover on the gas tank.

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:39.780
I looked every place I have ever had
one in any car I've ever been and

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:41.440
I couldn't find it.

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:45.460
And then I took out the manual in
the glove compartment to see if I could

00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:48.610
find it and then suddenly,
a miracle happened.

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:54.300
A car of a similar make drove up,
similar make, similar model.

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:58.160
So I ran up to the woman and I said,
this is a stupid question but

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:02.260
can you tell me where the button is
to open the cover on the gas tank?

00:10:02.260 --> 00:10:03.700
And she said there is none.

00:10:03.700 --> 00:10:06.846
I could have just going to over and
opened the cover and

00:10:06.846 --> 00:10:08.902
it would not have been a problem.

00:10:08.902 --> 00:10:13.456
Mainly, it's trivial, I wasted ten
minutes of my life, it's no big deal but

00:10:13.456 --> 00:10:15.440
it does show what happened.

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:20.930
I had the old answer, which is find the
button in order to get to the gas tank.

00:10:20.930 --> 00:10:23.610
The new question was how
do I open the cover?

00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:26.450
So I was really working
on the wrong problem and

00:10:26.450 --> 00:10:28.930
that's exactly what
happens a lot of times.

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:33.150
Let's talk a little bit more about it.

00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:36.530
If you look at something like
bike riding as an analogy,

00:10:36.530 --> 00:10:39.010
most people think they know
the rules of bike riding.

00:10:39.010 --> 00:10:41.238
But if you're going to ride
bikes at a higher level,

00:10:41.238 --> 00:10:45.030
there are rules which at
first seem counter-intuitive.

00:10:45.030 --> 00:10:48.940
For example, if you ride a bike
really well, you're not using your

00:10:48.940 --> 00:10:52.700
handlebars to steer the bike, you're
using your body weight to steer the bike.

00:10:52.700 --> 00:10:56.130
You don't sit on the seat if you want to
win the race, I got news for you.

00:10:56.130 --> 00:10:59.550
If you watch races,
they're not sitting on the seat and

00:10:59.550 --> 00:11:02.380
you're certainly not pushing on
the pedals, I'll tell you that much.

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:08.450
People have clips and they're pulling and
it's coming out of the rest of your body,

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:11.330
not the foot part,
it's the forces coming from within you.

00:11:11.330 --> 00:11:15.420
So these are three rules which in
bike riding seem counter-intuitive

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:16.690
if you're an amateur.

00:11:16.690 --> 00:11:19.450
I want to do the same thing
to you in problem solving.

00:11:19.450 --> 00:11:23.400
I want to give you a set of three rules
which are really important rules if you're

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:26.852
going to solve problems at a better level
then you've been doing it up till now.

00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:31.920
my three rules are,
don't use answers as questions,

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:33.780
I'll explain these in greater detail.

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:35.800
That's what the talk will be about.

00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:38.850
Don't use answers as
questions is the first rule.

00:11:38.850 --> 00:11:41.460
Don't use reasons is the second rule.

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:43.850
And don't confuse trying with doing.

00:11:43.850 --> 00:11:47.110
So those to me are the three
primary rules of

00:11:47.110 --> 00:11:49.650
trying to solve problems
at a higher level.

00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:54.170
And I'll now explain to you in
some detail what I mean by these.

00:11:55.870 --> 00:12:00.130
If you're stuck, really,
if you're literally stuck, the chances

00:12:00.130 --> 00:12:04.190
are very strong you're treating
a wrong answer as a right question.

00:12:04.190 --> 00:12:08.865
And that's universally true,
I've found that in my consulting work,

00:12:08.865 --> 00:12:11.650
I've found that in working
with PhD students.

00:12:11.650 --> 00:12:15.890
I find that in work with my family,
I find that in my job,

00:12:15.890 --> 00:12:19.687
I find that everywhere in my life,
that that's what happens.

00:12:21.070 --> 00:12:24.100
So the question is,
how do you get unstuck if you're stuck?

00:12:24.100 --> 00:12:29.440
If you got the gum under your shoe,
how do you get rid of it?

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:34.800
As I say, the rule is don't
use answers as questions.

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:35.930
What do I mean by that?

00:12:35.930 --> 00:12:37.340
Here's something that happened.

00:12:37.340 --> 00:12:43.660
This is a true story of I was working
with a large group of people,

00:12:43.660 --> 00:12:48.050
and I asked them,
what are you losing sleep over?

00:12:48.050 --> 00:12:52.600
And this one woman said,
I really can't find a spouse.

00:12:53.660 --> 00:12:58.376
I've tried everything,
I've gone on the Internet and

00:12:58.376 --> 00:13:01.090
all that stuff, she was stuck.

00:13:01.090 --> 00:13:04.850
So, the way I would unstuck her is,

00:13:04.850 --> 00:13:11.500
I would say what would it do for
you if you solved your problem?

00:13:11.500 --> 00:13:12.950
What is this the answer to?

00:13:12.950 --> 00:13:16.240
This is the answer to some problem.

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:18.975
What is finding a spouse the answer to?

00:13:18.975 --> 00:13:23.690
Or the way I said it to her is, what would
it do for you if you found a spouse?

00:13:23.690 --> 00:13:25.320
She said, I'd have companionship.

00:13:26.390 --> 00:13:30.460
So what happens then is we've gone to

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:35.540
getting companionship is really
the question she wants to work out.

00:13:35.540 --> 00:13:38.190
So the question is how does
she get companionship?

00:13:38.190 --> 00:13:40.170
Once she does that and forgets for

00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:44.830
the moment about finding a spouse,
the solution space opens up tremendously.

00:13:44.830 --> 00:13:51.080
Because how might I find a companion
has in addition to finding a spouse,

00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:57.740
meet friends online, take classes,
get a pet, join a club, go to jail, etc.

00:13:57.740 --> 00:14:00.690
So you can get companionship in lots and
lots of ways.

00:14:00.690 --> 00:14:04.960
So what's happened by reframing the
problem is you've opened up the solution

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:09.630
space and that's one of the magic
that's very important to understand.

00:14:09.630 --> 00:14:10.780
Now to do that,

00:14:10.780 --> 00:14:14.870
you have to be willing to let go of
the original finding a spouse idea.

00:14:14.870 --> 00:14:22.040
Now it's ironic because of I've
been married for a very long time,

00:14:22.040 --> 00:14:26.510
and one of the big issues in my life,
in my marriage, is companionship.

00:14:26.510 --> 00:14:29.410
because I'm busy talking to you on
the webinar and I'm doing a lot of other

00:14:29.410 --> 00:14:33.940
things, and I'm not giving my wife
as much companionship as I like.

00:14:33.940 --> 00:14:37.440
Since we've been married for over 60
years, I don't think she'll divorce me but

00:14:37.440 --> 00:14:38.980
it's an issue.

00:14:38.980 --> 00:14:43.430
So the idea is if this
woman found a spouse,

00:14:43.430 --> 00:14:47.680
he might not give her companionship,
he might be even worse than me.

00:14:47.680 --> 00:14:50.450
So she wouldn't be really
solving her problem, so

00:14:50.450 --> 00:14:53.040
what she's done is she's
taking an old answer,

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:58.410
which is get a spouse, for a new question,
which is really finding companionship.

00:14:58.410 --> 00:15:02.410
And if she reframes it,
it opens up and in fact,

00:15:02.410 --> 00:15:07.210
she might do better finding a spouse if
she wasn't so needy for companionship.

00:15:07.210 --> 00:15:08.490
So that's the basic idea.

00:15:08.490 --> 00:15:12.420
I'll give you an amusing version of
it which came up in a workshop I did,

00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:14.490
which I liked very much.

00:15:14.490 --> 00:15:19.760
This woman had the problem that her
boyfriend was snoring a lot and

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:21.250
she just couldn't handle it.

00:15:21.250 --> 00:15:23.520
They went to doctors, they had a surgery.

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:26.760
They did all sorts of medical things and
nothing worked.

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:31.980
So again, I said, what would it do for
you if your boyfriend stopped snoring?

00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:34.800
And she said,
I'd be able to get a good night's sleep.

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:38.920
Now the problem she should be
working on Is a good night's sleep,

00:15:38.920 --> 00:15:40.980
forget about stopping snoring.

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:45.720
And if you open it up and say, how do I
get a good night's sleep, there's many

00:15:45.720 --> 00:15:51.440
possibilities, including changing the
boyfriend, [LAUGH] which is a good one.

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:53.560
Or sleep in the other room or
earplugs, and

00:15:53.560 --> 00:15:57.990
so forth and so on, so the poor guy
could snore, and you could sleep.

00:15:57.990 --> 00:16:02.230
So it's again, it's this whole idea
of trying to solve the wrong problem.

00:16:02.230 --> 00:16:07.114
And even if he stopped snoring, maybe she
still wouldn't get a good night's sleep.

00:16:07.114 --> 00:16:09.239
Maybe he'd bother her in other ways,
I don't know.

00:16:09.239 --> 00:16:13.156
So the point simply is, if you know what
do we really want to be after make sure

00:16:13.156 --> 00:16:15.230
that's what you working on.

00:16:15.230 --> 00:16:21.320
Now this thing it's, and what happens is,
I hate to do this with Stanford freshmen.

00:16:21.320 --> 00:16:24.050
Because they say, but
then I didn't get a spouse.

00:16:24.050 --> 00:16:25.840
But I didn't stop snoring.

00:16:25.840 --> 00:16:28.030
They won't let go of the original problem.

00:16:28.030 --> 00:16:31.670
And it's a freshman syndrome, okay?

00:16:31.670 --> 00:16:33.869
Because once you're a graduate student and

00:16:33.869 --> 00:16:38.040
you realize in life it's much better
to disappear problems than solve it.

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:41.280
If you disappear your problem,
it's gone forever.

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:44.430
Whereas if you solve it,
it can come back and haunt you.

00:16:44.430 --> 00:16:45.820
So it's really interesting.

00:16:45.820 --> 00:16:48.820
It's okay to let go of
the original thing and

00:16:48.820 --> 00:16:52.550
not just cling to it because
that's what you chose originally.

00:16:52.550 --> 00:16:56.710
Okay, so in my case, I never found
the button to open that gas tank.

00:16:56.710 --> 00:16:58.160
I never found that button.

00:16:58.160 --> 00:17:01.790
But I'm sleeping very nicely,
because I had a full tank of gas and

00:17:01.790 --> 00:17:03.040
went on with my life.

00:17:03.040 --> 00:17:04.950
And that's analogous to everything.

00:17:04.950 --> 00:17:07.260
You have to be willing to
let go of the wrong thing,

00:17:07.260 --> 00:17:09.191
which was an answer to
the wrong problem all right.

00:17:10.240 --> 00:17:15.220
Now I'll give you some really powerful
examples of this is a project

00:17:15.220 --> 00:17:16.650
that some students did.

00:17:16.650 --> 00:17:19.720
It had to do with premature children.

00:17:19.720 --> 00:17:24.710
So, when a baby is born prematurely,
it must stay warm or it will die.

00:17:24.710 --> 00:17:27.350
It cannot manufacture
enough heat by itself.

00:17:27.350 --> 00:17:29.300
You need to do it externally and

00:17:29.300 --> 00:17:33.320
the say it's done in most of the world
is with things called incubators.

00:17:33.320 --> 00:17:35.410
We have these at the Stanford Hospital.

00:17:35.410 --> 00:17:40.160
You have them almost universally in
places where people have hospice.

00:17:40.160 --> 00:17:44.401
And a group of doctors
came to us from Nepal and

00:17:44.401 --> 00:17:49.570
they said look we have these incubators,
they're donated, they cost $20,000.

00:17:49.570 --> 00:17:53.550
They're donated from India but
we have some problems.

00:17:53.550 --> 00:17:57.290
One problem is they keep breaking and
we can't repair them.

00:17:57.290 --> 00:18:01.900
And the other is that you need
electricity, if there's no electricity,

00:18:01.900 --> 00:18:04.180
there's no warmth and the baby dies.

00:18:04.180 --> 00:18:08.550
And in Nepal the electricity
is intermittent at best.

00:18:08.550 --> 00:18:13.160
So, the problem they gave us, well,
could you keep fixed the incubators and

00:18:13.160 --> 00:18:14.460
make sure they work.

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:18.390
So, before the students went to Nepal
they work on battery backups to keep

00:18:18.390 --> 00:18:19.910
the electricity going.

00:18:19.910 --> 00:18:24.110
And to redesign it with fewer
parts to make it simpler.

00:18:25.640 --> 00:18:30.590
And then they went to Nepal and
they were horrified because they found out

00:18:30.590 --> 00:18:35.470
that the incubators are in the clinics.

00:18:35.470 --> 00:18:38.630
The clinics are in the villages,
in the towns.

00:18:38.630 --> 00:18:40.590
Many of the mothers are up in the hills,

00:18:40.590 --> 00:18:43.750
they can't get there in
time even if these worked.

00:18:43.750 --> 00:18:46.660
Even if these incubators were perfect and
they worked,

00:18:46.660 --> 00:18:50.450
the mothers can't leave their other
children alone, they let the baby die.

00:18:50.450 --> 00:18:52.200
They just can't get there in time.

00:18:52.200 --> 00:18:56.670
A lot of things indicated that this
was the wrong thing to be working on.

00:18:56.670 --> 00:18:57.390
Okay?

00:18:57.390 --> 00:19:01.840
So they reframed it, and they
understood they needed a point of view

00:19:01.840 --> 00:19:04.230
which took care of the mother and
the child.

00:19:04.230 --> 00:19:06.890
The question was, the mother and
the child were the user,

00:19:06.890 --> 00:19:09.210
the need was to keep the baby alive.

00:19:09.210 --> 00:19:14.020
And so they invented something that
looks like, well, you could just say

00:19:14.020 --> 00:19:18.660
the problem here in terms of reframing
it is make the machine work.

00:19:18.660 --> 00:19:21.940
That's the wrong problem,
the idea is to save the children.

00:19:21.940 --> 00:19:25.970
So if you save the children,
you can do other things.

00:19:25.970 --> 00:19:31.020
They came up with something which looks
like a sleeping bag and the magic is that

00:19:31.020 --> 00:19:36.240
is has inside of it a bladder which is
made of a kind of wax like material which

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:41.150
when you heat it, it melts and it
absorbs a lot of heat and it takes about

00:19:41.150 --> 00:19:45.680
5 hours to give off the heat, and it
keeps the baby at the right temperature.

00:19:45.680 --> 00:19:48.750
And you could melt it with anything,
you don't need electricity.

00:19:48.750 --> 00:19:52.260
You could use electricity, but you could
boil water, you could use the sun.

00:19:52.260 --> 00:19:58.760
So you melt the wax anyway you can,
and you're not relying on electricity.

00:19:58.760 --> 00:20:02.250
And it's saved over 300,000
babies at this point,

00:20:02.250 --> 00:20:06.330
and there was a nice moment when they
were invited to the old White House and

00:20:06.330 --> 00:20:09.590
this gentleman was nice enough
to say nice things about it.

00:20:09.590 --> 00:20:13.270
So it's an example of reframing
the problem to the right problem.

00:20:13.270 --> 00:20:15.500
In this case,
it wasn't fix the incubators.

00:20:15.500 --> 00:20:16.860
It was keep the babies alive.

00:20:17.940 --> 00:20:23.090
And another one, which is also
a classic one, has to do with someone,

00:20:23.090 --> 00:20:25.160
a friend of ours,
who works at General Electric.

00:20:25.160 --> 00:20:30.800
His name is Doug Dietz and he designs
MRI machines for children, he designed

00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:36.140
at one point, and what happened is he
designed the machine you can see there.

00:20:37.240 --> 00:20:41.640
And he went into a clinic
to see how it's going and

00:20:42.710 --> 00:20:44.890
he was told they loved his machine.

00:20:44.890 --> 00:20:51.385
The nurses loved his machine and then he
was so horrified to find out that about

00:20:51.385 --> 00:20:56.830
85% of the children have to be sedated
before they can go into the machine.

00:20:56.830 --> 00:21:00.590
They're so terrified by the machine.

00:21:00.590 --> 00:21:04.870
And he never saw the machine
from the point of a child.

00:21:04.870 --> 00:21:09.050
Where you're sort of asked to crawl
into the mouth of a metal monster.

00:21:09.050 --> 00:21:14.350
And he realized that really,
he ought to talk to some children.

00:21:14.350 --> 00:21:18.580
So he went around talking to children that
were chronic patients and all that, and

00:21:18.580 --> 00:21:24.080
when he found that when he talked to some,
one child that was cancer patient

00:21:24.080 --> 00:21:29.680
that her life was sort of devoid of
any adventure or any excitement,

00:21:29.680 --> 00:21:34.320
that her brother have who was well
you know have all these trophies and

00:21:34.320 --> 00:21:39.470
she had sort of a very spared life
because of her medical issues.

00:21:39.470 --> 00:21:43.080
And he figured well what you really
need in life is excitement and

00:21:43.080 --> 00:21:44.310
adventure and all that.

00:21:44.310 --> 00:21:50.280
So what he did is he didn't touch the
machines he just repainted them basically.

00:21:50.280 --> 00:21:54.120
And they made the machines as
part of an adventure series.

00:21:54.120 --> 00:21:58.610
So the top right one shows a pirate
ship and you have to hold still so

00:21:58.610 --> 00:22:00.370
the pirates don't find you.

00:22:00.370 --> 00:22:05.570
And the other one is on the water and
what he did is, before the children came,

00:22:05.570 --> 00:22:10.440
they made a comic book showing rays going
through your body, where you see bones.

00:22:10.440 --> 00:22:13.300
And they got nap sacks for the children.

00:22:13.300 --> 00:22:17.400
So they made it an adventure
rather than a medical experience.

00:22:17.400 --> 00:22:21.608
And the sedation rate dropped
down from 8.5% to about 1%.

00:22:21.608 --> 00:22:25.510
And she children were heard saying
mommy can we come back tomorrow.

00:22:26.540 --> 00:22:29.690
So what happened is he
reframed the problem again

00:22:29.690 --> 00:22:32.330
from one of does the machine work.

00:22:33.590 --> 00:22:37.860
Which it didn't because the children
were getting sedated all that.

00:22:37.860 --> 00:22:42.040
To one where not only to save them but
to give them an adventure,

00:22:42.040 --> 00:22:44.380
to give them something delightful.

00:22:44.380 --> 00:22:49.150
And he found that need by saying,
what was he really trying to do?

00:22:49.150 --> 00:22:51.980
So that's the idea, so
that's kind of rule one.

00:22:51.980 --> 00:22:54.200
Make sure you're working
on the right problem.

00:22:54.200 --> 00:22:57.320
Most of us solve problems all the time.

00:22:57.320 --> 00:23:00.680
We're always working on the right
problems, except when we're not.

00:23:00.680 --> 00:23:03.940
And when we're not, those
are the problems we're losing sleep over.

00:23:03.940 --> 00:23:06.220
And those are the problems to reframe.

00:23:06.220 --> 00:23:09.500
So if you're just going along your
life and you're solving problems, and

00:23:09.500 --> 00:23:11.080
everything is great, don't worry about.

00:23:11.080 --> 00:23:12.430
You don't need anything I've said.

00:23:12.430 --> 00:23:13.550
Forget about it.

00:23:13.550 --> 00:23:16.460
On the other hand, if you're losing
sleep and things are bad, and

00:23:16.460 --> 00:23:19.540
you don't know what to do, give it a shot.

00:23:19.540 --> 00:23:21.530
Reframe the problem.

00:23:21.530 --> 00:23:25.030
And simply the way to
reframe it is really simple.

00:23:25.030 --> 00:23:28.540
Just say what would it do for
me if I solve the problem?

00:23:28.540 --> 00:23:30.630
That will give you a new problem.

00:23:30.630 --> 00:23:33.940
Work on that new problem,
forget about the original one.

00:23:33.940 --> 00:23:37.000
It's magic,
it really works very very well, but

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:41.070
you have to be big enough not to
hang onto the original problem, and

00:23:41.070 --> 00:23:43.660
you have to be big enough
not to lie to yourself.

00:23:43.660 --> 00:23:47.350
The problem is most of us lie to
ourselves, we have a certain self-image,

00:23:47.350 --> 00:23:50.430
and we don´t tell ourselves the truth,
why we want to solve this problem.

00:23:51.570 --> 00:23:55.810
So you have to be truthful about what it
is you want to get out of the problem.

00:23:55.810 --> 00:23:59.140
And if you do that then you´re
going to do the right thing.

00:23:59.140 --> 00:24:01.550
So if this woman just said companionship.

00:24:01.550 --> 00:24:05.298
Because she just made that up and she
didn't want to tell me some other nastier

00:24:05.298 --> 00:24:09.750
reason she wants to get married like she
wants a rich guy to take care of her or

00:24:09.750 --> 00:24:12.430
whatever it is, it's not going to help.

00:24:12.430 --> 00:24:17.200
But if you tell yourself the truth then it
will help and you will often the solutions

00:24:17.200 --> 00:24:21.240
will be right out there in front of
you and often it almost solves itself.

00:24:21.240 --> 00:24:22.460
So that is the first rule.

00:24:22.460 --> 00:24:23.828
The 2nd rule is reasons.

00:24:23.828 --> 00:24:28.870
Did you ever ask yourself what's
the purpose of reasons in the world?

00:24:28.870 --> 00:24:30.880
Why do we have reasons?

00:24:30.880 --> 00:24:33.370
Well I've asked myself that and
I've thought about it a lot.

00:24:34.390 --> 00:24:37.070
And I've come up with
the following answer.

00:24:37.070 --> 00:24:41.020
The only use of reasons is
to seem to be reasonable.

00:24:42.050 --> 00:24:45.519
That's the only use of reasons is
to seem to be a reasonable person.

00:24:46.700 --> 00:24:48.662
So, for example if I did something.

00:24:48.662 --> 00:24:53.290
If I went over and I,
to some arbitrary person on the street and

00:24:53.290 --> 00:24:57.120
I kick them, and they said well,
why did you do that?

00:24:57.120 --> 00:24:59.870
I say I don't know,
I'm not a reasonable person.

00:25:01.190 --> 00:25:05.410
But if I'd kicked him and I said,
you reminded me of that kid in the fifth

00:25:05.410 --> 00:25:09.130
grade who used to bully me, then I have
a good reason for kicking him, right?

00:25:09.130 --> 00:25:10.470
So I'm a reasonable person.

00:25:10.470 --> 00:25:11.710
So that's absurd.

00:25:11.710 --> 00:25:15.020
But in general,
that's the level for most reasons.

00:25:15.020 --> 00:25:16.650
They're really not useful.

00:25:16.650 --> 00:25:20.120
But, and
who cares whether they can be harmful and

00:25:20.120 --> 00:25:23.960
that's the point about that so
I'll say a lot more about that right now.

00:25:25.190 --> 00:25:29.020
The first thing to understand is there's
no one reason for any human behavior.

00:25:30.800 --> 00:25:33.470
We are very complicated people and

00:25:33.470 --> 00:25:38.290
whatever we do takes into account our
history, our connection to other people,

00:25:38.290 --> 00:25:43.160
our connections to our friends, and
it's not just the thing itself, so

00:25:43.160 --> 00:25:48.020
if someone asks me, Bernie will you
do me a favor and do something?

00:25:50.020 --> 00:25:52.530
I might say yes, and
if you say well why'd you do that?

00:25:52.530 --> 00:25:54.820
I say, well he asked me to do it.

00:25:54.820 --> 00:25:58.970
But if a different person asks me I might
say no, with all the same circumstances.

00:25:58.970 --> 00:26:00.658
Because of my history with the person.

00:26:00.658 --> 00:26:06.920
So our lives are very complicated, we go
way back in the past in terms of our DNA,

00:26:06.920 --> 00:26:11.896
and how we do things never
is one specific thing.

00:26:11.896 --> 00:26:15.470
So what happens, is if someone asks
you a reason, or you need a reason for

00:26:15.470 --> 00:26:19.550
yourself, you'll pick the one
reason that makes you look good.

00:26:19.550 --> 00:26:23.340
You'll pick the one reason
that makes you look okay.

00:26:23.340 --> 00:26:28.030
So for example, whenever I give this talk,
at this point I notice my mouth is dry,

00:26:28.030 --> 00:26:30.310
which it is at this moment, okay?

00:26:30.310 --> 00:26:33.030
And if you say to me, well,
why is your mouth dry?

00:26:33.030 --> 00:26:34.710
I'll say, well, I've been talking a lot.

00:26:34.710 --> 00:26:37.000
That seems reasonable, right?

00:26:37.000 --> 00:26:40.910
But I don't tell you that last night
I had a bottle of wine for dinner.

00:26:40.910 --> 00:26:43.730
I don't tell you that I'm
always chronically dehydrated.

00:26:43.730 --> 00:26:45.810
My wife says I never drink enough.

00:26:45.810 --> 00:26:49.790
So I don't tell you all of those other
things in my history which would lead

00:26:49.790 --> 00:26:54.800
to me not necessarily having a dry
mouth even if I talked a lot.

00:26:54.800 --> 00:26:55.390
Okay?

00:26:55.390 --> 00:26:58.150
So what I do is I take the one
simple thing in this very

00:26:58.150 --> 00:26:59.880
complicated set of things.

00:26:59.880 --> 00:27:01.440
I don't know the reason my mouth is dry.

00:27:01.440 --> 00:27:03.430
It just is dry, okay?

00:27:03.430 --> 00:27:05.960
And all these things are true
that I just said to you, and

00:27:05.960 --> 00:27:09.310
a few others I don't want to
tell you contribute to it.

00:27:09.310 --> 00:27:12.120
So I pick the one,
which is I'm talking a lot.

00:27:12.120 --> 00:27:13.310
Fine.
And that's reasonable.

00:27:13.310 --> 00:27:14.600
And who cares?

00:27:14.600 --> 00:27:18.930
The problem with that is
often reasons are an excuse.

00:27:18.930 --> 00:27:20.860
And if you use them as excuses,

00:27:20.860 --> 00:27:23.690
they don't let you accomplish
what you want to accomplish.

00:27:23.690 --> 00:27:27.240
And by using the reason,
you let yourself off the hook.

00:27:27.240 --> 00:27:31.010
My epiphany on this many years ago, I was

00:27:32.870 --> 00:27:37.750
on the board of directors of a company in
Berkeley and I would periodically go to

00:27:37.750 --> 00:27:42.990
the board meeting by driving from
Stanford, Palo Alto on the freeway

00:27:42.990 --> 00:27:48.400
to Berkeley to the meeting off
the Berkeley campus somewhere.

00:27:48.400 --> 00:27:53.380
And I was always late I
was late in every meeting.

00:27:53.380 --> 00:27:58.030
And when I came in I said there was
a large traffic on the highway.

00:27:58.030 --> 00:28:00.680
I came in I said, gee,
I'm really sorry I'm late.

00:28:00.680 --> 00:28:04.134
There's so much traffic on
those days it was highway 17.

00:28:04.134 --> 00:28:05.985
So much traffic on Highway 17.

00:28:05.985 --> 00:28:09.575
And they say, it's okay, Bernie,
as long as nothing happened.

00:28:09.575 --> 00:28:12.895
But then I realized it wasn't okay
because these people have lives too, and

00:28:12.895 --> 00:28:16.235
I was being abusive, and
I was keeping them waiting.

00:28:16.235 --> 00:28:21.520
And at one point I realized that I
should either get there on time,

00:28:21.520 --> 00:28:23.837
or I should leave the board.

00:28:23.837 --> 00:28:27.375
And I realized in a way that my
behavior was insulting to them because

00:28:27.375 --> 00:28:29.933
I wasn't giving them
enough valance in my life.

00:28:29.933 --> 00:28:34.390
I wasn't giving them enough,
it wasn't important.

00:28:34.390 --> 00:28:38.180
What was important was the last few phone
calls I took before there was no cell

00:28:38.180 --> 00:28:39.370
phones those days.

00:28:39.370 --> 00:28:42.160
The last few phone calls I took
in my office before I left.

00:28:42.160 --> 00:28:44.600
The teletype message I had to send out,

00:28:44.600 --> 00:28:47.370
the conversation I had with
a colleague in the hall.

00:28:47.370 --> 00:28:51.700
And by the time I got to my car,
if there was no traffic in Palo Alto,

00:28:51.700 --> 00:28:55.100
no traffic on the highway, no traffic
in Berkeley, I would have made it.

00:28:55.100 --> 00:28:57.210
But there was traffic,
so I didn't make it.

00:28:57.210 --> 00:28:59.110
And I blamed the traffic.

00:28:59.110 --> 00:29:02.440
But of course, the realization was
I was not leaving early enough.

00:29:02.440 --> 00:29:05.924
I wasn't giving it enough
valence as I say in my life.

00:29:05.924 --> 00:29:10.640
Once I had that, I changed my life from
becoming the person who was always late

00:29:10.640 --> 00:29:14.760
for everything, to the pain, the backside
that starts everything on time,

00:29:14.760 --> 00:29:17.520
that's always on time, and is never late.

00:29:17.520 --> 00:29:18.830
And it's really simple.

00:29:18.830 --> 00:29:21.360
It makes my life so
much simple in terms of driving.

00:29:21.360 --> 00:29:26.760
I don't have to do near death experiences
that come only five minutes late and

00:29:26.760 --> 00:29:28.130
stuff of that nature.

00:29:28.130 --> 00:29:32.250
And this is simply that once I was
willing to tell myself the truth and

00:29:32.250 --> 00:29:35.880
not say it's the traffic,
I could handle it.

00:29:35.880 --> 00:29:37.140
It's simple as that.

00:29:37.140 --> 00:29:39.509
So that's a really important
thing to understand.

00:29:40.610 --> 00:29:48.890
And basically I think that the best
position to have is that reasons are ****.

00:29:48.890 --> 00:29:51.366
All reasons are basically ****.

00:29:51.366 --> 00:29:54.619
Even if you don't intend them to be
that way because you don't know.

00:29:54.619 --> 00:29:58.489
I mean the people put subjects
into MRI machines, and

00:29:58.489 --> 00:30:04.400
they've had them dictate an action,
and then give a reason for the action.

00:30:04.400 --> 00:30:07.910
And it turns out the parts
of the brain that fires

00:30:07.910 --> 00:30:10.560
first is the one that does the action.

00:30:10.560 --> 00:30:13.180
The part that comes up
with the reason is after.

00:30:13.180 --> 00:30:16.550
So basically you do what you do, and
then you make up a reason for it.

00:30:16.550 --> 00:30:18.460
Most of the time you're in automatic,
most of it.

00:30:18.460 --> 00:30:19.610
You don't think about walking,

00:30:19.610 --> 00:30:21.990
you don't think about breathing,
you don't think about talking.

00:30:21.990 --> 00:30:24.560
You just talk, it comes out.

00:30:24.560 --> 00:30:26.420
So then you say, why'd you say that?

00:30:26.420 --> 00:30:29.270
Then I'll think up a reason why I said it,
I just said it.

00:30:29.270 --> 00:30:32.550
I don't know why I said it,
and it's the same thing.

00:30:32.550 --> 00:30:34.936
You don't know the reasons for
these things and

00:30:34.936 --> 00:30:38.224
it's supposed to be reasonable
to have a reason for everything.

00:30:38.224 --> 00:30:39.832
But it doesn't work that way.

00:30:39.832 --> 00:30:42.965
I don't know if you've had this
experience, I've had it many times.

00:30:42.965 --> 00:30:46.556
I'd make up my mind to do something and
then suddenly I'm doing something else.

00:30:46.556 --> 00:30:48.924
You know all the time.

00:30:48.924 --> 00:30:51.875
Like people make up
they're going to die and

00:30:51.875 --> 00:30:56.186
then they're eating candy or
they do this and they do wrong.

00:30:56.186 --> 00:30:59.331
So you're not in control,
I got news for you.

00:30:59.331 --> 00:31:02.076
There's something very
complicated controlling so

00:31:02.076 --> 00:31:05.750
the reasons are just to cover for
being out of control basically.

00:31:05.750 --> 00:31:09.128
So basically, if you just keep
the idea that reasons are ****,

00:31:09.128 --> 00:31:10.860
it's really an important thing.

00:31:12.730 --> 00:31:16.520
So, it's better to keep
silence than add ****.

00:31:16.520 --> 00:31:17.970
You don't need the reasons.

00:31:17.970 --> 00:31:20.840
You often do not,
no one cares what the reasons are.

00:31:20.840 --> 00:31:25.170
It's a question of what you did or what
you didn't do, not what the reason is.

00:31:25.170 --> 00:31:27.060
The reason's irrelevant.

00:31:27.060 --> 00:31:28.190
Okay?

00:31:28.190 --> 00:31:32.002
So the main thing is do
not try this at home.

00:31:32.002 --> 00:31:34.290
I am just fearful that
you're going to go home and

00:31:34.290 --> 00:31:37.785
tell everyone in your life their
reasons are ****, that doesn't work.

00:31:37.785 --> 00:31:41.370
I've never once told my wife her reasons
are ****, I've never once told my

00:31:41.370 --> 00:31:45.190
children their reasons are ****, I've
never once told my biking buddies their

00:31:45.190 --> 00:31:48.870
reasons are ****, I've never once told
anyone I work with their reasons are ****.

00:31:48.870 --> 00:31:52.110
It's the best way possible
to ruin a relationship.

00:31:52.110 --> 00:31:54.940
But the person you can tell is yourself.

00:31:54.940 --> 00:31:59.240
And it's really important to tell
yourself that way and to just know that.

00:31:59.240 --> 00:32:03.540
And if you say something,
you realize it's ****, It's okay but

00:32:03.540 --> 00:32:06.020
you say to yourself,
I'm never going to do that again.

00:32:06.020 --> 00:32:08.890
And you'll do it again but
eventually you'll stop.

00:32:08.890 --> 00:32:12.980
Whereas if you just keep doing it and
don't notice it, you'll never stop.

00:32:12.980 --> 00:32:17.242
So if you want to change your behavior,
if you want to improve, just keep in mind

00:32:17.242 --> 00:32:22.043
that the reason you just said it, even
with the best of intentions, is pure ****.

00:32:22.043 --> 00:32:24.300
So don't give reasons
unless you have to and

00:32:24.300 --> 00:32:28.581
don't be a jerk if someone says what's
the reason for this, tell them something.

00:32:28.581 --> 00:32:29.268
Okay?

00:32:29.268 --> 00:32:32.390
But if you can, do not give reasons.

00:32:32.390 --> 00:32:36.660
I have this experience
where I get maybe four or

00:32:36.660 --> 00:32:41.720
five emails a month from students in,
nowadays, it's China or

00:32:41.720 --> 00:32:46.960
Iran or Pakistan, who want to come
to Stanford to do a PhD with me.

00:32:46.960 --> 00:32:50.970
And some of them are really well thought
out messages and they've researched me and

00:32:50.970 --> 00:32:52.680
all that and I could ignore them.

00:32:52.680 --> 00:32:55.620
I don't owe them anything but
I felt uncomfortable.

00:32:55.620 --> 00:33:02.360
So what I used to do is give them some
good reason, I can't do it, some excuse.

00:33:02.360 --> 00:33:04.700
I'm sorry, I don't have any money.

00:33:04.700 --> 00:33:07.210
Or I'm going on sabbatical.

00:33:07.210 --> 00:33:09.960
And whenever I gave them a reason,
they pushed back.

00:33:09.960 --> 00:33:12.770
So if I don't have any money,
they say I have a rich uncle.

00:33:12.770 --> 00:33:15.510
If I'm going on sabbatical,
I can wait for you, professor.

00:33:15.510 --> 00:33:18.330
And then we go on and
on until I just truncate it and

00:33:18.330 --> 00:33:19.860
I had a bad feeling about it.

00:33:19.860 --> 00:33:24.110
Now that I have this epiphany about
reasons, I don't give them reasons.

00:33:24.110 --> 00:33:27.640
I get the email, I say, sorry,
can't help you, good luck.

00:33:28.670 --> 00:33:33.290
And what happens is about 90% of the time,
get back an email saying, thank you for

00:33:33.290 --> 00:33:35.090
answering my email, professor.

00:33:35.090 --> 00:33:36.560
It's the end of the story.

00:33:36.560 --> 00:33:38.710
It's such a good example.

00:33:38.710 --> 00:33:39.690
They don't need a reason.

00:33:39.690 --> 00:33:42.530
They just want to know,
are you going to let me in or you're not.

00:33:42.530 --> 00:33:43.660
Once they know, it's over.

00:33:43.660 --> 00:33:44.620
The drama is over.

00:33:44.620 --> 00:33:46.430
They'll move on [INAUDIBLE].

00:33:46.430 --> 00:33:48.765
And it's really important
to keep that in mind.

00:33:48.765 --> 00:33:53.380
Do not use reasons unless
you really have to, okay?

00:33:53.380 --> 00:33:56.840
So that's the second rule, okay?

00:33:56.840 --> 00:33:59.490
And it's really important.

00:33:59.490 --> 00:34:01.450
We have a kind of funny thing.

00:34:01.450 --> 00:34:05.950
We have a thing, people I work with,
we all know this idea,

00:34:05.950 --> 00:34:10.920
if someone gives you reason, for

00:34:10.920 --> 00:34:13.770
example, we have some faculty member
who'll get up in a meeting and say,

00:34:13.770 --> 00:34:17.450
well, the reason I can't do it is
that the dean said blah, blah, blah.

00:34:17.450 --> 00:34:22.330
That's a good reason with a lot
of o's in there, like five or

00:34:22.330 --> 00:34:24.310
six o's, meaning it's ****.

00:34:24.310 --> 00:34:28.110
So the code for
us is we just say that's a good reason.

00:34:28.110 --> 00:34:31.010
And you can do that for
yourself, it really helps.

00:34:31.010 --> 00:34:32.220
If you give yourself a reason for

00:34:32.220 --> 00:34:36.480
something you have,
just tell yourself that's a good reason.

00:34:36.480 --> 00:34:40.380
And you'll get a lot of insight
because it's ****, okay.

00:34:40.380 --> 00:34:45.860
And finally, we come to the third rule,
which is not to confuse trying with doing,

00:34:45.860 --> 00:34:48.580
okay, and
that's kind of an interesting thing.

00:34:48.580 --> 00:34:50.770
Now, if you're a fan of Star Wars,

00:34:50.770 --> 00:34:55.380
you're quivering at this point because
you don't know that Yoda was wrong.

00:34:55.380 --> 00:34:58.910
I always prove at this point, I'm afraid
I'm going to be struck dead, but So

00:34:58.910 --> 00:35:00.360
far I've gotten away with it.

00:35:00.360 --> 00:35:02.480
There is a try, there is a try.

00:35:02.480 --> 00:35:05.510
I know what they were saying in the movie,
I have no problem with that but

00:35:05.510 --> 00:35:09.050
in real life there is a try and
there's nothing wrong with trying.

00:35:09.050 --> 00:35:11.500
Trying is okay, it's just not doing.

00:35:11.500 --> 00:35:15.140
It's just something else,
it's another activity which is good and

00:35:15.140 --> 00:35:18.090
there are all sorts of different
activities which are okay,

00:35:18.090 --> 00:35:22.700
one of which is called doing and it's
important not to confuse all these other

00:35:22.700 --> 00:35:26.420
things Which looked like it was doing and
let me explain that to you.

00:35:28.000 --> 00:35:33.340
So to do you need to have an intention, so
first well what is your intention, okay?

00:35:33.340 --> 00:35:36.210
If you don't have any intention
it's just a pipe dream.

00:35:36.210 --> 00:35:40.790
Pipe dream refers to people who
used to smoke opium in dens and.

00:35:40.790 --> 00:35:45.210
Smoke a pipe with opium and dreamt
about all sorts of wonderful things.

00:35:45.210 --> 00:35:46.700
So we all have wonderful dreams.

00:35:46.700 --> 00:35:47.380
We all have pipe dreams.

00:35:47.380 --> 00:35:48.660
There's nothing wrong with it.

00:35:48.660 --> 00:35:52.200
Pipe dreams are wonderful,
even if you don't have opium.

00:35:52.200 --> 00:35:53.670
It's wonderful to have pipe dreams.

00:35:53.670 --> 00:35:56.020
You have dreams whatever they are.

00:35:56.020 --> 00:35:57.760
It's great to have all sorts of fantasies.

00:35:57.760 --> 00:35:59.130
There's nothing wrong with it.

00:35:59.130 --> 00:36:00.840
But that's not your intention.

00:36:00.840 --> 00:36:02.330
That's just a dream.

00:36:02.330 --> 00:36:03.160
Okay this one.

00:36:05.190 --> 00:36:08.620
And if you have something that you
really have intention of doing,

00:36:08.620 --> 00:36:11.590
are you willing to give it attention?

00:36:11.590 --> 00:36:13.810
So you need these two things,

00:36:13.810 --> 00:36:19.060
you need to have intention and
you need to have attention.

00:36:19.060 --> 00:36:21.480
So if you have an intention
to do something,

00:36:21.480 --> 00:36:23.890
it's not going to happen
unless you give it attention.

00:36:23.890 --> 00:36:25.880
Attention it requires.

00:36:25.880 --> 00:36:29.690
There is a word that not many people
know even if you are a native speaker,

00:36:29.690 --> 00:36:35.600
it is called VELLEITY and a lot of people
experience velleity, now what velleity

00:36:35.600 --> 00:36:40.810
means is is the least possible effort
you can possibly put into something.

00:36:40.810 --> 00:36:44.790
So you have a real good idea,
I am going to start business.

00:36:44.790 --> 00:36:46.840
And I'll think about it for a while and

00:36:46.840 --> 00:36:51.860
maybe I'll do something tomorrow,
that's velleity.

00:36:53.250 --> 00:36:56.850
Velleity is just,
it's one step beyond the pipe dream.

00:36:56.850 --> 00:37:00.630
You slither, going to implement, but it's
a little too much to get out of bed, or

00:37:00.630 --> 00:37:01.730
two much work.

00:37:01.730 --> 00:37:04.130
So that's velleity, all right?

00:37:04.130 --> 00:37:08.310
So that's no attention, if you have
velleity, you don't have attention.

00:37:08.310 --> 00:37:12.910
So I think you could make a little chart,
which I made for you to see.

00:37:12.910 --> 00:37:17.550
And on one axis, under the vertical axis,
I plot intention.

00:37:17.550 --> 00:37:20.860
The horizontal axis, I plot attention.

00:37:20.860 --> 00:37:24.040
And we can talk about
four states of being.

00:37:24.040 --> 00:37:26.460
The lover left state is a pipe dream,

00:37:26.460 --> 00:37:32.030
where you have very little attention and
very little intention.

00:37:32.030 --> 00:37:33.910
So it's something you just dream about.

00:37:33.910 --> 00:37:35.700
You have no intention or attention.

00:37:35.700 --> 00:37:41.100
If you go up on the left hand side to the
top one, you have something where you have

00:37:41.100 --> 00:37:46.769
an intention But there's no
attention being given to it, that's.

00:37:46.769 --> 00:37:54.050
You have an intention to do it, but
you don't give it any attention.

00:37:54.050 --> 00:37:58.600
And then on the right hand side on
the bottom, you have what's called trying.

00:37:58.600 --> 00:38:01.960
Trying is when you have
an intention to do something.

00:38:01.960 --> 00:38:04.560
And you give it a little attention.

00:38:04.560 --> 00:38:06.110
You give it a little attention.

00:38:06.110 --> 00:38:07.190
And it might work.

00:38:07.190 --> 00:38:08.270
It might work.

00:38:08.270 --> 00:38:09.210
It might be simple.

00:38:09.210 --> 00:38:09.840
It might work.

00:38:09.840 --> 00:38:10.430
And it's fine.

00:38:10.430 --> 00:38:10.990
It'll happen.

00:38:10.990 --> 00:38:12.280
It might happen.

00:38:12.280 --> 00:38:15.590
On the other hand,
if it requires a lot more attention, or

00:38:15.590 --> 00:38:19.800
you encounter an obstacle or
something, you don't do it.

00:38:19.800 --> 00:38:20.519
It doesn't happen.

00:38:22.270 --> 00:38:26.410
And then finally on the top quadrant
on the right, we have doing.

00:38:26.410 --> 00:38:29.330
Doing requires both intention and
attention.

00:38:29.330 --> 00:38:33.920
You have to intend to do it and you have
to give it the attention it requires.

00:38:33.920 --> 00:38:38.000
Now all four states,
as far as I'm concerned, are fine.

00:38:38.000 --> 00:38:41.270
There's nothing wrong with pipe dreams,
there's nothing wrong with velleity,

00:38:41.270 --> 00:38:44.060
there's nothing wrong with trying,
there's nothing wrong with doing.

00:38:44.060 --> 00:38:46.080
It could even be better to try.

00:38:46.080 --> 00:38:49.720
I'll give you an example
that happened in my life.

00:38:49.720 --> 00:38:51.520
My wife hates when I tell the story but

00:38:51.520 --> 00:38:56.740
I think it's perfect and
she's not listening so I'll tell you.

00:38:56.740 --> 00:38:58.960
We were coming home from San Francisco and

00:38:58.960 --> 00:39:03.370
we passed a movie theatre called the Roxy
which is a kind of funky theater

00:39:03.370 --> 00:39:07.020
in the Mission District that shows
movies you don't see anywhere else.

00:39:07.020 --> 00:39:11.140
And I noticed the line around the rock
was bigger than I'd ever seen,

00:39:11.140 --> 00:39:12.750
it was going around the block.

00:39:12.750 --> 00:39:18.260
And I realized it was a movie about
a musical group and the group was there.

00:39:18.260 --> 00:39:19.580
I had never heard of them.

00:39:19.580 --> 00:39:22.460
But clearly,
all these people there must be great.

00:39:22.460 --> 00:39:24.190
So I say to my wife,
let's go to the movies.

00:39:24.190 --> 00:39:25.860
She said, no, I'm tired, let's go home.

00:39:25.860 --> 00:39:27.490
I said, come on, let's go to the movies.

00:39:27.490 --> 00:39:28.730
She finally relented.

00:39:28.730 --> 00:39:29.940
She said, okay, let's go.

00:39:29.940 --> 00:39:34.650
I said, okay, jump out of the car, buy the
tickets, I'll go find the parking space.

00:39:34.650 --> 00:39:38.270
Ten minutes later, I come back and
my wife is not in line.

00:39:38.270 --> 00:39:40.300
She's just standing in
front of the ticket booth.

00:39:40.300 --> 00:39:42.010
I said, well, how come you're not in line?

00:39:42.010 --> 00:39:44.480
She said, they're sold out.

00:39:44.480 --> 00:39:45.960
I said, well, how come you're not online?

00:39:45.960 --> 00:39:46.880
She said, didn't you hear me?

00:39:46.880 --> 00:39:48.360
They're sold out.

00:39:48.360 --> 00:39:50.770
I said, yeah, but you stay here.

00:39:50.770 --> 00:39:54.460
And I went along the line, and
I got two tickets somehow.

00:39:54.460 --> 00:39:56.300
And we went to the show.

00:39:56.300 --> 00:40:01.378
And it's a great example,
when my wife was trying So

00:40:01.378 --> 00:40:05.070
I'd say awareness or
mindfulness is the key.

00:40:05.070 --> 00:40:06.950
It's a good reason, they're sold out.

00:40:06.950 --> 00:40:09.450
It's a good reason not to go, right, so.

00:40:09.450 --> 00:40:13.510
I was doing, didn't matter, sold out or
not, I was going to get those tickets,

00:40:13.510 --> 00:40:15.770
come hell or high water, I was doing.

00:40:15.770 --> 00:40:18.180
Now it turns out in this
case it was terrible.

00:40:18.180 --> 00:40:20.435
Trying would have been
much better than doing.

00:40:20.435 --> 00:40:22.740
[LAUGH] You never know.

00:40:22.740 --> 00:40:24.620
Sometimes you can get killed doing.

00:40:24.620 --> 00:40:27.090
So I'm not voting, I'm not voting.

00:40:27.090 --> 00:40:30.680
And really in life, sometimes, it's
really fun to try and it doesn't happen.

00:40:30.680 --> 00:40:34.450
But it's a difference between trying and
doing, you understand that?

00:40:34.450 --> 00:40:38.880
I have an example where I was supposed to
go to a meeting that I didn't want to go

00:40:38.880 --> 00:40:41.230
to, I was sort of obligated to go to it.

00:40:41.230 --> 00:40:44.220
It was in Dallas, Fort Worth, and

00:40:44.220 --> 00:40:49.830
I came to the San Francisco airport,
and it was a miracle.

00:40:49.830 --> 00:40:54.451
All the flights to Dallas, Fort Worth were
cancelled because there was a snow storm.

00:40:54.451 --> 00:40:57.270
So I called up the people, I said I'm
sorry, I can't come, it's snowing.

00:40:57.270 --> 00:40:59.590
They said, we understand,
thanks, and all that.

00:40:59.590 --> 00:41:04.310
Now the truth is, if I wanted to,
if my life depended on getting to Dallas,

00:41:04.310 --> 00:41:08.680
Fort Worth, snow storm or not, airport
closed or not, I would have gotten there.

00:41:09.730 --> 00:41:15.300
So I'd say awareness or
mindfulness is the key.

00:41:15.300 --> 00:41:18.693
It didn't happen, it was great, it was the
best thing in the world that I was trying.

00:41:18.693 --> 00:41:21.414
But if I was doing,
it wouldn't have stopped me.

00:41:21.414 --> 00:41:22.880
And that illustrates the whole idea.

00:41:22.880 --> 00:41:26.460
You will, and when you're doing
stuff you'll have obstacles.

00:41:26.460 --> 00:41:31.560
But the magic comes in walking around the
obstacle and not letting it deter you, and

00:41:31.560 --> 00:41:36.560
often you get to a much better solution
than you would if you didn't do that.

00:41:36.560 --> 00:41:38.550
So it's important to
understand the difference.

00:41:38.550 --> 00:41:42.500
And, again, I'm not voting on
any of the four, I'm saying just

00:41:42.500 --> 00:41:46.880
know which state you want to be in and
don't confuse which one you are.

00:41:46.880 --> 00:41:50.230
And you can go from one to the others,
no problem.

00:41:50.230 --> 00:41:54.470
If I have to kill someone in order to do,
and it's against my moral principles,

00:41:54.470 --> 00:41:56.760
I'm not going to do it,
I'm going to change my mind.

00:41:56.760 --> 00:41:59.056
I'm going to stop, I'm going to try, or

00:41:59.056 --> 00:42:03.570
I'm going to go have a pipe dream,
I don't care what.

00:42:03.570 --> 00:42:04.940
You can go from one to the other,

00:42:04.940 --> 00:42:08.520
just know which state you're
in is the important message.

00:42:08.520 --> 00:42:12.250
Okay, so if you're really doing,
an obstacle doesn't stop you.

00:42:13.460 --> 00:42:18.110
So I'd say awareness or
mindfulness is the key.

00:42:18.110 --> 00:42:23.559
So I'd say awareness or
mindfulness is the key.

00:42:24.920 --> 00:42:28.760
And the other things that could happen,
and I'm just going to close with this.

00:42:28.760 --> 00:42:31.060
Language shapes the way we think, and

00:42:31.060 --> 00:42:33.970
it's important to worry
about what language you use.

00:42:33.970 --> 00:42:38.100
So for example, instead of using
the word but, if you use the word and

00:42:38.100 --> 00:42:41.210
a lot more,
you'll find you'll have a better life.

00:42:41.210 --> 00:42:45.150
Instead of saying you have to, if you say
you want to, you'll have a better life.

00:42:45.150 --> 00:42:51.220
Instead of saying you can't,
you can say you will not.

00:42:51.220 --> 00:42:55.400
And instead of saying help,
you can use the word assist, and so forth.

00:42:55.400 --> 00:42:59.190
There's a whole bunch of words you can
use that make your life much better.

00:42:59.190 --> 00:43:01.467
The words change the way you are,

00:43:01.467 --> 00:43:06.360
and if you use the right words it will
make things go a lot better for you.

00:43:06.360 --> 00:43:09.885
So I've kind of given you all
the wisdom I have at the moment,

00:43:09.885 --> 00:43:12.322
and I want to thank you for
your attention.

00:43:12.322 --> 00:43:14.332
And we're opening up now for

00:43:14.332 --> 00:43:19.250
the second part of this session
where we'll have questions.

00:43:19.250 --> 00:43:22.390
Thank you.
&gt;&gt; And now we'll head into our Q&amp;A

00:43:22.390 --> 00:43:25.180
session and we'll take some
questions from our attendees.

00:43:26.320 --> 00:43:30.450
So Bernie, our first question is, how can
you convince your management to rephrase

00:43:30.450 --> 00:43:35.421
a problem without giving reasons?
&gt;&gt; Well usually

00:43:35.421 --> 00:43:40.280
it's a matter of doing,
rather than giving reasons.

00:43:40.280 --> 00:43:45.921
And I had an experience where
three people from a company,

00:43:45.921 --> 00:43:52.380
I probably shouldn't name it,
came and did one of our workshops.

00:43:52.380 --> 00:43:56.256
And they went back and had an idea,
and the manager said I'm sorry,

00:43:56.256 --> 00:43:58.970
we have no room to do this, and all that.

00:43:58.970 --> 00:44:02.250
And he gave them all sorts of
reasons why they couldn't do it.

00:44:02.250 --> 00:44:06.190
And they said well, they caucused and

00:44:06.190 --> 00:44:10.300
they said well, let's do it,
not let's try to do it.

00:44:10.300 --> 00:44:14.960
And they set up a space in the hallway,
he wouldn't give them an office,

00:44:14.960 --> 00:44:19.370
they set up a space in the hallway and
they eventually did it and they went on.

00:44:19.370 --> 00:44:23.900
So it's just a matter of, it's a good
reason that the manager doesn't

00:44:23.900 --> 00:44:25.910
want you to do it,
really if you look in your heart.

00:44:25.910 --> 00:44:28.580
There are lots of stories around.

00:44:28.580 --> 00:44:33.055
I think Emerson's got the cell phones
because, which was a big hit for

00:44:33.055 --> 00:44:37.355
them years ago, because this one guy
his boss said he couldn't do it so

00:44:37.355 --> 00:44:41.205
he went home in the garage and
he made the first cell phone for Emerson.

00:44:41.205 --> 00:44:46.935
So it really gets to that, it's really
it's a good reason the boss and

00:44:46.935 --> 00:44:49.455
the dean and all that,
and if you want to do it

00:44:49.455 --> 00:44:53.010
you are smart enough to find a way.
&gt;&gt; Great,

00:44:53.010 --> 00:44:56.523
it's kind of like the old saying, where
there's a will, there's a way, right?

00:44:56.523 --> 00:44:57.227
&gt;&gt; That's right, yeah.

00:44:57.227 --> 00:44:59.139
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and so for our next question,

00:44:59.139 --> 00:45:02.777
with regard to the lack of gender
diversity, and private equity, and

00:45:02.777 --> 00:45:07.110
hedge funds, and unconscious gender
biases, how would you reframe the problem?

00:45:08.400 --> 00:45:11.928
That problem in specific [LAUGH].
&gt;&gt; Well the question is,

00:45:11.928 --> 00:45:13.480
what's the problem?

00:45:13.480 --> 00:45:18.010
You're just telling me a social
situation which you don't like.

00:45:18.010 --> 00:45:22.080
Is it how do I get more women in society?

00:45:22.080 --> 00:45:25.840
You haven't really stated your problem,
you've stated a big issue.

00:45:25.840 --> 00:45:31.900
So first of all, the best thing would be
to just take one person, like yourself or

00:45:31.900 --> 00:45:38.470
whoever you want, and just like I did
with the woman with the money thing.

00:45:38.470 --> 00:45:43.530
And just specifically say who it is,
what the issue is, and what

00:45:43.530 --> 00:45:47.640
would happen if you could find a solution
without worrying about the solution, okay?

00:45:47.640 --> 00:45:51.460
And then,
that would give you a way to work on it.

00:45:51.460 --> 00:45:54.270
We don't have a problem yet to reframe.

00:45:54.270 --> 00:45:58.610
But if you gave me a specific problem,
let's say,

00:45:58.610 --> 00:46:01.820
let's make one up,
how do I get a higher salary?

00:46:02.960 --> 00:46:07.490
So, I would say, what would it do for
you if you got a higher salary?

00:46:08.570 --> 00:46:11.970
It might be, it will give me more
respect in the organization.

00:46:11.970 --> 00:46:14.940
So the question is, how do I get more
respect in the organization rather

00:46:14.940 --> 00:46:16.640
than the higher salary,
if it's not working.

00:46:16.640 --> 00:46:20.840
I have nothing against you going for
a higher salary, hopefully you get it.

00:46:20.840 --> 00:46:22.170
But that's what I'm saying.

00:46:22.170 --> 00:46:24.367
If you take a specific question,

00:46:24.367 --> 00:46:28.694
not a general world issue like
peace forever and stuff like that.

00:46:28.694 --> 00:46:32.875
If you take a specific problem and
say that and you actually have attempted

00:46:32.875 --> 00:46:36.510
to solve it, you give it attention and
it's not solving.

00:46:36.510 --> 00:46:38.860
Then, it's time to reframe it, and

00:46:38.860 --> 00:46:43.770
you reframe it by saying what
would the solution to that do for

00:46:45.200 --> 00:46:48.670
me?
&gt;&gt; So on the topic of problem solving it

00:46:48.670 --> 00:46:53.747
requires a great attitude and perspective,
how do we practice it every day?

00:46:53.747 --> 00:46:54.485
What are some tips for

00:46:54.485 --> 00:46:55.557
practicing every day?
&gt;&gt; Sure,

00:46:55.557 --> 00:46:57.350
well you're practicing it
every day like it or not.

00:46:57.350 --> 00:47:00.170
You're either doing a good job at it or
a bad job at it.

00:47:00.170 --> 00:47:05.460
So I would say, the word mindfulness
which is a little overused is the answer.

00:47:05.460 --> 00:47:09.910
It's a matter of not being oblivious but
looking at what you do, and

00:47:09.910 --> 00:47:12.910
maybe going, I don't know if you
keep a diary or something like that.

00:47:12.910 --> 00:47:18.020
But a good thing might be to go
when you go home at night or

00:47:18.020 --> 00:47:21.070
in the morning if you
work on the night shift.

00:47:21.070 --> 00:47:26.900
Write down what happened during the day in
terms of incidents and problems and what.

00:47:26.900 --> 00:47:31.360
Analyze them, and say what you will
never do again, and what you did wrong.

00:47:31.360 --> 00:47:36.110
So be aware of your behavior and see how
it goes ,and use that as a way to learn.

00:47:36.110 --> 00:47:39.863
And you know we say failure's
a great way to learn.

00:47:39.863 --> 00:47:43.258
So if you really do something bad,
you've learned,

00:47:43.258 --> 00:47:46.509
make a, underline it in red and
don't do it again.

00:47:46.509 --> 00:47:51.142
You'll do it again but eventually you'll
stop if you keep taking notice of it.

00:47:51.142 --> 00:47:55.094
So I'd say awareness or
mindfulness is the key.

00:47:55.094 --> 00:47:59.128
And don't go unconscious about
what's happening every day.

00:47:59.128 --> 00:47:59.853
&gt;&gt; So I'd say awareness or

00:47:59.853 --> 00:48:00.620
mindfulness is the key.

00:48:01.660 --> 00:48:03.160
Shifting on to the next question.

00:48:03.160 --> 00:48:06.920
Are there any ways to help yourself
move from the trying to doing stage?

00:48:06.920 --> 00:48:10.950
Any ideas to find the motivation to
keep working even though there are many

00:48:10.950 --> 00:48:18.073
obstacles in the way?
&gt;&gt; If you're not motivated, why bother?

00:48:18.073 --> 00:48:23.052
[LAUGH] Many years ago, I was in Bulgaria,
and I heard this wonderful saying.

00:48:23.052 --> 00:48:27.205
The Bulgarian saying,
there's no need to do anything.

00:48:27.205 --> 00:48:28.995
And why is that?

00:48:28.995 --> 00:48:31.175
If it's really important,
someone else will do it.

00:48:31.175 --> 00:48:32.800
If it's not important, why bother?

00:48:32.800 --> 00:48:36.205
[LAUGH] So
there's a lot of wisdom in that.

00:48:36.205 --> 00:48:39.365
If it's not worth doing,
it's not worth doing well at all.

00:48:40.780 --> 00:48:44.240
So just this whole idea that you have to
do, you have to do, you don't have to do.

00:48:44.240 --> 00:48:46.320
You can have a great time just trying it.

00:48:46.320 --> 00:48:49.440
But if you are motivated,

00:48:49.440 --> 00:48:54.400
if you really want to do something, then
you can't let the obstacles block you.

00:48:54.400 --> 00:48:57.220
My favorite story is my friend, Paul.

00:48:57.220 --> 00:49:03.210
He was a swimmer, and
he had an accident in the shower, and he

00:49:03.210 --> 00:49:08.240
had a back surgery and the surgeon said,
you can never swim competitively again.

00:49:08.240 --> 00:49:13.470
But there was an Olympics coming up and
he wanted to go, and he talked to some

00:49:14.700 --> 00:49:18.870
guy who was of a guru type and
he said, who can stop you?

00:49:18.870 --> 00:49:21.770
If you want to do it, do it,
who cares what the doctor said?

00:49:21.770 --> 00:49:27.330
So he trained fiercely,
there was not much time.

00:49:27.330 --> 00:49:30.930
Trained fiercely in a local reservoir
here called Lexington Reservoir,

00:49:30.930 --> 00:49:33.710
and he just qualified.

00:49:33.710 --> 00:49:36.220
He was like the last person
to get onto the team.

00:49:36.220 --> 00:49:39.420
It was between him and
someone, he just got on.

00:49:39.420 --> 00:49:43.840
And he went to the Olympics and he won
a gold medal and he set a world record.

00:49:43.840 --> 00:49:47.430
He came back, he put the medal on
the doctor's desk, and that was the point.

00:49:47.430 --> 00:49:49.220
He had a good reason not to go.

00:49:49.220 --> 00:49:51.894
The doctor said it won't happen.

00:49:51.894 --> 00:49:55.420
But he was so motivated, and
he's been a serial entrepreneur.

00:49:55.420 --> 00:49:58.970
He tells me every time he does something,
there's always a blockage.

00:49:58.970 --> 00:50:04.210
There's always like the accident and
getting around is where the magic is.

00:50:04.210 --> 00:50:05.780
But he has the motivation.

00:50:05.780 --> 00:50:08.370
If you don't have the motivation,
forget about it.

00:50:08.370 --> 00:50:14.420
Do something you feel motivated about.
&gt;&gt; So, last question here.

00:50:14.420 --> 00:50:18.780
This is a quite specific question but what
is the approach or the right problem to

00:50:18.780 --> 00:50:24.310
solve if you're disliking your current
job and want a new job opportunity,

00:50:24.310 --> 00:50:29.470
how can you rephrase that problem?
&gt;&gt; I don't understand,

00:50:29.470 --> 00:50:32.120
it seems like you have this problem,
it's all solved.

00:50:32.120 --> 00:50:33.770
I don't know if there is
anything to rephrase.

00:50:33.770 --> 00:50:37.450
If you don't like your job and
you want to get a new job opportunity,

00:50:37.450 --> 00:50:40.840
I'd say that the question is how
do I get a new job opportunity?

00:50:40.840 --> 00:50:44.740
And if you have any problems doing that,
say what would it do for me?

00:50:44.740 --> 00:50:49.360
And if it's I'd have a happier life or
happy thing, maybe

00:50:49.360 --> 00:50:54.220
you could stay where you are and reframe
what you're doing to make it happier.

00:50:54.220 --> 00:51:00.020
You may have certain constraints that
aren't really there, and some people,

00:51:00.020 --> 00:51:03.870
I have a friend who's
chronically unhappy and

00:51:03.870 --> 00:51:06.614
every job he goes to he
has problems with it.

00:51:06.614 --> 00:51:11.425
[LAUGH] So it doesn't matter if he changes
his job, now he has to change his being.

00:51:11.425 --> 00:51:18.860
So it there's that whole aspect to it,
but if you're really in a job that's bad,

00:51:18.860 --> 00:51:22.840
there's no hope and all that, then
clearly the answer is to change your job.

00:51:22.840 --> 00:51:25.080
And you'll find lots of
reasons why you can't,

00:51:25.080 --> 00:51:29.660
but they're all just good reasons.
&gt;&gt; Moral of the story,

00:51:29.660 --> 00:51:32.122
there are a lot of good reasons.

00:51:32.122 --> 00:51:36.670
[LAUGH] So thank you very much,
Bernie, for this awesome talk.

00:51:36.670 --> 00:51:39.790
We're at the top of the hour now and
it's time to wrap up.

00:51:39.790 --> 00:51:43.660
If you have any additional questions about
Stanford's professional programs on design

00:51:43.660 --> 00:51:48.360
thinking and innovation, please don't
hesitate to reach out to us directly and

00:51:48.360 --> 00:51:52.370
make sure to check your inbox for the
webinar recording within the next week.

00:51:52.370 --> 00:51:54.757
Thank you again for
joining us today and hopefully,

00:51:54.757 --> 00:51:57.374
we can see many of you with us
here on campus in September.

